course: dictionary
lesson: 8630
title: Scuffle: a short, messy fight
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scuffle is a short, confused fight with pushing and grabbing. It is usually not very serious, so it is not the same as a big battle. «Josh had been in a scuffle and was scared to say.» The word scuffle tells you the fight was messy and small, and Josh was worried about it.
explain: Scuffle can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, it is the fight itself: «Then came the scuffle.» As a verb, it means to fight this way: «At break Maddie and Jake both got into the same scuffle over the football.» Look for the clues around the word: pushing, grabbing, a football, a muddy knee.
explain: Scuffle can also mean to walk while dragging your feet, or to make a scraping noise. «There are scuffling feet and tiny voices outside.» The feet are not fighting here. Ask yourself: does the sentence show a fight, or people moving with a scraping sound? The clues decide the meaning.
fact: A scuffle is a short, confused fight with pushing and grabbing.
fact: «A brief scuffle» means a fight that lasted only a short time.
fact: To scuffle along means to walk dragging your feet, or to make a scraping noise.
fact: News reports and school incident reports often use «a scuffle broke out» to describe a small fight.
fact: A quick check: swap in the word fight and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Josh had been in a scuffle and was scared to say.» What is a scuffle? | A big football match with a referee | A loud song sung at school | A long, quiet walk home | *A short, messy fight with pushing and grabbing
exercise: «There are scuffling feet and tiny voices outside.» What does scuffling mean here? | Flying up into the sky | Singing loudly together | Fighting a long war | *Moving while dragging the feet and making a scraping noise
exercise: Nadia says scuffle is just a posh word for fight. Why might a writer choose scuffle? | It means the same as a huge war | It means a happy party | It is a longer word | *It shows the fight was short, messy and not very serious
test: The Rat hears «scuffling feet» outside the door. What is scuffling here? | Swimming in a pond | Eating a sardine | *Walking while dragging the feet and scraping
test: Which best describes a scuffle? | A quiet talk in the library | A long race around the park | A huge battle with armies | *A brief, messy fight with pushing and grabbing
exam: Yesterday Josh and Sam ___ over the ball at break. | scuffle | scuffling | scuffles | *scuffled
